# RG-3 finite-q scope correction

The continuum parity-fiber model has state space `S1 x Z2`. In that declared
continuum model the parity sum on a fixed circular ordering is exactly the
one-dimensional Ising-cycle partition factor and is independent of the angular
gap lengths.

For the original finite even-q phase orbit, however, parity is not an
independent coordinate: the phase labelled by `n` has angle `2*pi*n/q` and
parity `(-1)^n`. Thus the exact finite-q law consists of two shifted `q/2`
angular grids, not an independent product `S1 x Z2`.

The product-fiber statement is therefore an **asymptotic bridge**, not an exact
finite-q identity. It is justified for smooth observables by the shifted
trapezoid convergence already used in the q-extension proofs. For an M-cell
ordering, the additional scaling `q/M^2 -> infinity` suppresses phase-grid
collisions and makes the one-step even/odd angular offset negligible relative
to the random inter-cell spacing with probability tending one.

This correction does not change the continuum `Z2` factorization theorem or
the conditional RG-3 joint limit, but it weakens any wording that claimed
exact finite-q parity/angle independence.
